The order of the global symbols in asm6x's .symtab - probes, 2026-09-30

What is left between ASM6x's objects and asm6x 8.2.2's is the order of the non-local
symbols in .symtab (and the .rel* and .strtab that follow from it). These probes were
assembled on the Windows box (`sh probe.sh <dir>`: cl6x 8.2.2, `-c --symdebug:none`,
under C:\cxx1\symorder), and globals.py reads the global part of an object's .symtab
back; <name>.order beside each object is that list. The rule was NOT found in the time
box; what was measured is below, and it rules out most of the obvious rules.

Measured

1. The order does not depend on the order of mention for names that do not tie
   (p1/perm0..7: the same six names in eight orders, one output; p1/def6*: .global'ed
   or defined in either order, one output). What matters is the name.
2. It is a strict order on names, with occasional ties broken later-mentioned-first
   (p5: two-symbol files in both mention orders - 20 of 22 pairs come out the same way
   round whichever was mentioned first; e277/e99 flips, so those two tie and the one
   mentioned later is written first, i.e. a chain pushed at the front). So: a hash
   table walked from its top bucket down (or a sort on a hash), chains prepended.
3. Single-character names come out in descending character order, always: z..a _ Z..A
   (p4/ruler, p1/ref40). Two-character names xd (x a letter, d a digit) come out by x
   descending then d descending (p4/rall), and the whole v0..z9 block sits above z, the
   whole a0..u9 block below A - 210 names between two adjacent single characters, so
   this is not a monotone function of the name mod anything small.
4. The last character of a five-letter name enters by XOR: in p2/ee300 every group
   eeAB0..eeAB9 comes out 9 8 1 0 3 2 5 4 7 6, which is descending (F ^ d) with F's low
   nibble 6 - for every prefix eeAB; four-letter e2Bd and shorter groups come out 9..0.
   No linear weight w with (B + d*w) mod N produces that ten (checked, and shown
   impossible by hand), so the digit is XORed in. The only rotate-XOR step whose low
   nibble is 6 after four steps from 'e' and 0 after fewer is h = rotl(h,7) ^ c (or
   rotl 23), the classic rotating hash - but see 6.
5. Ties measured: e277 = e99, and in the 300-name files e2Dx ties e(D+2)(x+2) for every
   D and x (e266/e88, e202/e24, ...); p2/f300 shows blocks of ten alternating
   (f259..f250 f39..f30 f249..f240 f29..f20 ...) with no ties. A candidate must
   reproduce both.
6. What does not fit, even one 24-name file (p2/rep: a x1..12 and b x1..12), under
   "bucket descending, ties allowed": every polynomial h*k+c, h*k^c, (h+c)*k, (h^c)*k,
   h<<k (+|^) c, rotl(h,k) (+|^) c, rotr, PJW/ELF, sdbm, FNV-1/1a, djb2, with k to 300
   (1100 for the plain polynomial), seed 0 or the length, reduced as u32, u31, |int32|,
   u16, u15, |int16|, h>>16, h^(h>>16), h>>8, h&0xffffff, and N from 2 to 70000
   (fit3.cpp); rotate-XOR/ADD with N to 2^26 (fitrot.cpp). An open-addressing table
   (linear probe either way, walked either way) over the same hashes was simulated
   exactly on ee300 (sim.cpp) and did not finish its N range in the time box - and
   finding 2 says chaining, not probing.

What to try next

- The hash is XOR-based with a rotation or an odd multiplier (4), the walk is
  descending (3), chains are prepended (2). Missing is the reduction to a bucket and/or
  a seed: seeds other than 0 and the length; h % N with N above 2^26 or the value sorted
  whole; a table whose size grows and REHASHES (a chain's order after a rehash is the
  reverse of the old walk, which could give f300's alternating blocks without ties);
  hashing the name as 4-byte words.
- p5's two-symbol probe is a cheap tie/order oracle: two files per question, no
  reasoning about wraps. The ruler files (p4) place a block of ten between known
  singles.
